From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jonathan Schwender <schwenderjonathan@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 14:07:48 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] docs: Base ohos-main on Servo releases

ohos-main follows Servo's releases from now on instead of upstream's main
branch, so that each sync lands on a version that upstream has published.
Decision 1 of the runbook now takes the newest upstream release tag as the
base, and CONTRIBUTING.md says so.

A release tag is not on `upstream/main`: Servo cuts a release branch at the
commit that bumps the version and adds release commits to it, for 0.8.0
one that takes Stylo from crates.io and one that stops publishing
servo-svg. Hence:

- Step 0 fetches the tag, lists its release commits, which must change no
  code, and checks the merge-queue run of its branch point. That run is
  also the upstream run that servo-ohos-wpt shows next to ohos-main's,
  since it takes the merge base of `main` and the tested revision.
- `check_integrity.py` accepts a base that is a servo/servo release tag, as
  well as an ancestor of `main`.
- Stylo's `update.sh` vendors from either Stylo source (the Stylo patch),
  and step 1 says that a vendored copy of another version than the
  required one leaves Cargo building the unpatched crate.
- Step 1 checks `Cargo.lock` after every commit with `rebase --exec`: on
  0.8.0, two patches' lock files merged cleanly but kept the old version
  of the workspace crate they add.
- `check_stack.py`, and so the `integrity` job, fails on a
  `[[patch.unused]]` entry in `Cargo.lock`, which a `[patch]` table that
  doesn't match its dependency leaves: Cargo would build the unpatched
  crate.

Validation: for v0.8.0 (4e136d8ef31), `check_integrity.py`'s compare against `main` reports "diverged" and its tag lookup finds v0.8.0, so the base passes; a commit that is no tag is not found. The runbook's step 0 for v0.8.0 lists the two release commits (Stylo from crates.io, servo-svg not published) and the branch point a19af13eeb9, whose merge-queue run succeeded. `check_integrity.py` on the folded stack: 132 patches, the patch set reproduces the tip, both vendored copies are up to date, and "base ok: servo/servo release v0.8.0". Negative control of the `[[patch.unused]]` check: with the Stylo entries under `[patch."https://github.com/servo/stylo"]` again, `cargo metadata` leaves eight `[[patch.unused]]` entries in Cargo.lock, and `check_vendored()` fails.
AI-assisted: Yes (agent session; written by the agent during the 2026-11-09 sync, on the decision to follow Servo releases)
Signed-off-by: Jonathan Schwender <schwenderjonathan@gmail.com>
